WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:11.790
And if you're ready for our next speaker — it's Milovan Dekić,

2
00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:14.730
who will tell us about design thinking.

3
00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:19.390
Milovan, by the way, is the co-author of a book on this

4
00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:24.150
and head of the user research team at Nordeus.

5
00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:28.530
There you go, the stage is yours.

6
00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:44.740
Thank you for coming. Thanks to the organizers for the invitation.

7
00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:53.500
So, my name is Milovan Dekić, and the idea is to talk today about what's called design thinking.

8
00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:58.660
Before we start, tell me, can you raise your hand:

9
00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:04.160
who among you has experience with design thinking? Cool.

10
00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:14.420
There aren't many of us. Okay. So, instead of theorizing here now, because the topic is very broad and unwieldy,

11
00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:18.460
I'll just try to highlight a few main lessons,

12
00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:26.240
that is, some things that — if you want to solve some problem, or someone's problem, or create some product —

13
00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:33.540
basically some hints you can use to perhaps make better decisions

14
00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:36.300
when solving this problem or creating the product.

15
00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:45.800
Yes, this was written by my colleague Marko Jevtić, an executive producer at Nordeus, and me,

16
00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:50.500
we wrote together a book called "Design Thinking: How to Create Products People Need."

17
00:01:54.420 --> 00:02:01.580
To start, just one example. What does design thinking insist on?

18
00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:09.400
Imagine we put ourselves in IBM's place, which was developing voice recognition software.

19
00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:15.680
How can it be done? Sit down and develop voice recognition software.

20
00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:24.660
However, that can be expensive, hard, and in the end you might make something people don't need.

21
00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:31.640
And then design thinking, basically, advises trying to make a prototype

22
00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:36.200
or somehow fake, imitate this experience

23
00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:41.240
and see how the end user reacts to it.

24
00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:44.680
If he reacts badly, that is, if you're making something he doesn't need,

25
00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:47.060
then you know in advance that perhaps it's not worth doing.

26
00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:55.000
So at IBM, basically, when they started working on speech recognition software,

27
00:02:56.020 --> 00:03:01.200
a person just had to speak into a microphone, and text basically appears on the screen.

28
00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:03.280
And everyone's like: top class.

29
00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:05.420
But how did it actually happen?

30
00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:11.300
It happened like this: a typist sat hidden in a corner, and what he said, she typed.

31
00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:14.660
That is, you didn't develop software, you faked the experience.

32
00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:18.980
And then the person who tries it can tell you whether they like it or not.

33
00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:25.140
So that's one of the things, actually one of the examples of design thinking.

34
00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:30.820
Try to simulate the experience you want to create before rushing straight into developing it.

35
00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:35.920
So, basically, design thinking is when you can gather as a team

36
00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:40.640
and try to creatively solve some problem you have,

37
00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:45.840
and the main goal, basically, is to solve the user's problem or satisfy some need of theirs.

38
00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:50.200
So, design thinking has several phases.

39
00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:51.860
And here's the first hint, or first idea.

40
00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:57.760
If you want to make something for someone, say a mobile game or anything, or some software —

41
00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:02.060
the people who will use it — spend a bit of time with them.

42
00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:06.020
See what they do, how they do it, and talk with them a little.

43
00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:07.440
That's the idea of empathy.

44
00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:12.520
To go back to the well-known example of Airbnb: when they started, not a single soul rented apartments.

45
00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:18.400
When the owners put themselves in the place of both the guys who rent out an apartment

46
00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:22.900
and those who need to rent one, they realized the whole experience was a disaster.

47
00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:27.780
First, the people renting out apartments had no quality cameras at all to photograph

48
00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:30.900
those apartments of theirs; on the other hand, someone looking at a lousy photo

49
00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:32.180
obviously won't want to rent.

50
00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:37.940
And then someone from Y Combinator told them: go talk to your users.

51
00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:43.940
Then they understood, and after some changes their business jumped 400%.

52
00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:47.080
So, basically, that's what design thinking advises you.

53
00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:50.480
Now I'll give you an example in the form of a video.

54
00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:54.600
So it would be good to try listening to it, it lasts five minutes.

55
00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:56.940
I don't know whether anyone has heard what "milkshake marketing" is.

56
00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:03.020
Clayton Christensen — I think the translation of his book "The Innovator's Dilemma" comes out today —

57
00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:04.840
worked for McDonald's.

58
00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:10.340
McDonald's called him in, and they said: okay, how do we improve the milkshakes we sell?

59
00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:12.100
We want more money, frankly.

60
00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:15.280
Let's increase sales by 15 or 20%.

61
00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:17.740
What's the first idea that comes to mind?

62
00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:22.000
Add more sugar, for example.

63
00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:27.880
Okay, now, instead of retelling it, I'd like us to listen to a video

64
00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:31.160
where Christensen explains what they actually did,

65
00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:33.620
and it's a perfect example of design thinking.

66
00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:34.640
Play the video, please.

67
00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:35.340
Well, does anyone have an idea,

68
00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:36.860
based on everything he said?

69
00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:37.660
So, what he's saying —

70
00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:38.820
in marketing there's this:

71
00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:39.960
when people buy a drill,

72
00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:41.800
what they need isn't the drill as such,

73
00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:42.900
but a hole in the wall.

74
00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:46.920
And what could McDonald's do then?

75
00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:10.140
They made it thicker,

76
00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:11.420
so that it would actually last

77
00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:11.860
longer,

78
00:11:12.020 --> 00:11:12.640
if you have

79
00:11:14.840 --> 00:11:16.240
a longer commute to work.

80
00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:17.900
So, basically,

81
00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:20.180
this helps you

82
00:11:20.180 --> 00:11:21.300
get around your intuition:

83
00:11:21.400 --> 00:11:22.060
well, honestly,

84
00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:22.940
let's add some other flavor —

85
00:11:22.940 --> 00:11:23.700
and sales aren't going up.

86
00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:26.200
What design thinking says:

87
00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:27.740
go and look at those people

88
00:11:28.940 --> 00:11:31.140
who actually hire the product

89
00:11:31.220 --> 00:11:32.040
to get a job done.

90
00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:34.320
And here's another important takeaway,

91
00:11:34.340 --> 00:11:35.380
I think, in that

92
00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:40.600
your main competitor

93
00:11:40.600 --> 00:11:41.520
doesn't have to be

94
00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:42.340
from your field at all.

95
00:11:42.620 --> 00:11:43.420
I don't know who's heard

96
00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:43.700
say,

97
00:11:43.780 --> 00:11:44.960
Reed Hastings from Netflix.

98
00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:47.180
Who's Netflix's main competitor?

99
00:11:57.310 --> 00:11:57.950
Cable, yes.

100
00:11:58.350 --> 00:11:59.390
Well, they go a bit further,

101
00:11:59.450 --> 00:12:00.010
he says: sleep.

102
00:12:01.790 --> 00:12:03.250
Or drinking a bottle of wine.

103
00:12:06.310 --> 00:12:08.050
Or everything else you do

104
00:12:08.050 --> 00:12:09.150
when you're not watching Netflix.

105
00:12:10.690 --> 00:12:11.630
Fortnite — I mean,

106
00:12:11.750 --> 00:12:13.410
even in their official report

107
00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:15.170
it says that last year

108
00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:16.330
they lost to Fortnite.

109
00:12:19.490 --> 00:12:20.370
But the first idea is this:

110
00:12:20.510 --> 00:12:21.670
spend a little time with these people,

111
00:12:21.790 --> 00:12:22.470
talk to them,

112
00:12:22.610 --> 00:12:23.550
observe them,

113
00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:25.250
and this will probably open up for you

114
00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:25.930
some perspectives.

115
00:12:26.750 --> 00:12:27.630
Defining the problem.

116
00:12:28.270 --> 00:12:30.790
When people tell you something,

117
00:12:30.970 --> 00:12:34.170
when you observe how they perceive things,

118
00:12:36.010 --> 00:12:37.470
again, you need to understand

119
00:12:37.470 --> 00:12:39.330
what the problem is that the person has.

120
00:12:39.730 --> 00:12:42.450
So, when you're waiting for an elevator —

121
00:12:42.730 --> 00:12:44.050
imagine you have a company

122
00:12:44.050 --> 00:12:44.990
where people tell you:

123
00:12:44.990 --> 00:12:46.410
the elevator is slow.

124
00:12:47.730 --> 00:12:48.750
What's the solution?

125
00:12:50.430 --> 00:12:51.250
A faster elevator.

126
00:12:51.390 --> 00:12:52.110
Music, right.

127
00:13:00.270 --> 00:13:01.370
Design thinking advises you:

128
00:13:01.430 --> 00:13:02.370
if you talk to these people,

129
00:13:03.350 --> 00:13:04.010
five whys —

130
00:13:04.390 --> 00:13:05.290
ask them five times:

131
00:13:05.350 --> 00:13:06.310
why is this a problem for you?

132
00:13:06.550 --> 00:13:08.230
It can be rather agonizing

133
00:13:08.350 --> 00:13:09.710
if you really interview people

134
00:13:09.710 --> 00:13:10.230
this way,

135
00:13:10.310 --> 00:13:11.830
I've experienced it a billion times.

136
00:13:14.710 --> 00:13:15.710
But you'll realize that their problem

137
00:13:15.710 --> 00:13:16.430
may actually be

138
00:13:16.430 --> 00:13:17.710
that they actually have nothing to do.

139
00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:19.750
Which again leads to the conclusion

140
00:13:19.750 --> 00:13:20.970
that you don't need to pour

141
00:13:20.970 --> 00:13:22.230
I don't know how many thousand euros

142
00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:22.890
into a faster elevator,

143
00:13:23.310 --> 00:13:25.430
but you only need to make the waiting experience

144
00:13:25.430 --> 00:13:26.590
less agonizing

145
00:13:26.590 --> 00:13:27.470
or less boring.

146
00:13:28.270 --> 00:13:29.130
And one startup, say,

147
00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:30.110
did this in Germany.

148
00:13:31.270 --> 00:13:32.070
There are loads of examples:

149
00:13:32.150 --> 00:13:32.870
when you're waiting for a bus

150
00:13:33.030 --> 00:13:34.050
and you're nervous —

151
00:13:34.150 --> 00:13:34.630
that is, what's the problem,

152
00:13:34.630 --> 00:13:35.630
say, when the bus isn't there?

153
00:13:36.350 --> 00:13:37.590
What could the solution be?

154
00:13:37.590 --> 00:13:38.910
More buses

155
00:13:38.910 --> 00:13:40.110
or faster buses,

156
00:13:40.330 --> 00:13:41.590
but in fact the problem may be

157
00:13:41.590 --> 00:13:42.610
just your anxiety —

158
00:13:42.610 --> 00:13:44.230
that you don't know when it will come.

159
00:13:44.430 --> 00:13:45.510
And then this idea

160
00:13:45.510 --> 00:13:46.510
of showing

161
00:13:46.510 --> 00:13:48.190
"in so many minutes" is great,

162
00:13:48.190 --> 00:13:50.230
because it solves the anxiety problem.

163
00:13:52.030 --> 00:13:53.330
The problem might be:

164
00:13:53.330 --> 00:13:54.490
I'm bored waiting.

165
00:13:54.910 --> 00:13:56.050
And then, okay,

166
00:13:56.190 --> 00:13:57.050
you can do anything,

167
00:13:57.130 --> 00:13:58.590
but what this startup did

168
00:13:58.590 --> 00:13:59.130
was: okay,

169
00:13:59.250 --> 00:14:02.270
let's make the experience of waiting for transport

170
00:14:02.270 --> 00:14:03.250
interesting.

171
00:14:03.470 --> 00:14:04.470
And then they made

172
00:14:06.210 --> 00:14:07.150
a ping-pong game

173
00:14:08.130 --> 00:14:09.430
from two different sides.

174
00:14:12.110 --> 00:14:14.450
Whenever people tell you something,

175
00:14:14.770 --> 00:14:16.370
try to question them further

176
00:14:16.370 --> 00:14:17.870
about what the problem really is.

177
00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:19.050
What's the problem, that you're waiting?

178
00:14:19.130 --> 00:14:20.110
Well, I'm bored,

179
00:14:20.130 --> 00:14:21.110
or I'm nervous.

180
00:14:21.810 --> 00:14:22.950
The whole point is

181
00:14:22.950 --> 00:14:25.190
that when you understand this problem well,

182
00:14:25.710 --> 00:14:27.110
much better solutions,

183
00:14:27.110 --> 00:14:28.230
and very often,

184
00:14:28.350 --> 00:14:29.510
maybe cheaper solutions

185
00:14:29.510 --> 00:14:30.770
can come to mind

186
00:14:30.770 --> 00:14:31.730
than those complex ones

187
00:14:31.730 --> 00:14:33.150
that solve some complicated,

188
00:14:33.880 --> 00:14:36.230
and perhaps even false problem.

189
00:14:36.870 --> 00:14:38.790
So this, again, is what

190
00:14:38.790 --> 00:14:39.570
design thinking advises.

191
00:14:40.170 --> 00:14:40.750
Idea generation.

192
00:14:42.190 --> 00:14:42.810
Idea generation

193
00:14:43.450 --> 00:14:44.070
implies —

194
00:14:44.350 --> 00:14:45.710
we're probably all familiar with this,

195
00:14:45.810 --> 00:14:46.590
each of us

196
00:14:46.590 --> 00:14:48.630
has been at least once at some workshop

197
00:14:49.350 --> 00:14:50.930
where we generate ideas.

198
00:14:51.270 --> 00:14:52.570
Well, I think we need this,

199
00:14:52.710 --> 00:14:53.510
this, this.

200
00:14:53.510 --> 00:14:55.430
The whole point of this brainstorming

201
00:14:55.430 --> 00:14:56.530
is to do it

202
00:14:56.530 --> 00:14:57.330
systematically

203
00:14:59.340 --> 00:15:01.160
and for you to go far in breadth.

204
00:15:02.500 --> 00:15:03.160
What, say,

205
00:15:03.260 --> 00:15:03.960
is a pretty good example?

206
00:15:13.720 --> 00:15:14.100
But, say,

207
00:15:14.180 --> 00:15:15.520
what's interesting about Fortnite?

208
00:15:17.920 --> 00:15:18.900
What's the first thing that comes to mind

209
00:15:18.900 --> 00:15:20.220
when you go into some shooter game,

210
00:15:20.220 --> 00:15:21.640
whether PUBG or Fortnite:

211
00:15:21.760 --> 00:15:22.980
like, you go to fight,

212
00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:23.840
competitively,

213
00:15:23.920 --> 00:15:24.360
you want to win,

214
00:15:24.440 --> 00:15:25.200
you want to be first.

215
00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:26.600
Fortnite changed a lot

216
00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:27.980
in the competitive genre:

217
00:15:27.980 --> 00:15:29.440
the point is no longer at all

218
00:15:29.440 --> 00:15:29.980
about being first,

219
00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:31.080
but about having a good time.

220
00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:33.000
Just good fun.

221
00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:36.760
All this is good fun.

222
00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:37.760
And then they said:

223
00:15:37.760 --> 00:15:39.340
okay, what else could be fun?

224
00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:40.320
Well, it could be fun

225
00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:41.620
to throw a party in the game.

226
00:15:41.860 --> 00:15:42.720
And in the end

227
00:15:42.720 --> 00:15:43.320
it got to the point

228
00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:44.340
that in Fortnite

229
00:15:44.340 --> 00:15:45.660
there's a separate party mode.

230
00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:47.300
And then others copy.

231
00:15:50.400 --> 00:15:51.340
This, again, I think,

232
00:15:51.400 --> 00:15:51.760
couldn't have

233
00:15:51.760 --> 00:15:53.320
just come to your mind like that,

234
00:15:53.320 --> 00:15:54.700
because if you just stick to

235
00:15:54.700 --> 00:15:56.380
your basic assumptions —

236
00:15:56.460 --> 00:15:57.200
well yes, people are here

237
00:15:57.200 --> 00:15:58.000
to shoot,

238
00:15:58.140 --> 00:15:58.820
to be first —

239
00:15:58.980 --> 00:16:00.880
and that's mostly intuition,

240
00:16:01.580 --> 00:16:02.920
if you think relatively superficially

241
00:16:02.920 --> 00:16:03.900
about the problem.

242
00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:05.120
And here you really

243
00:16:05.120 --> 00:16:05.800
need to try hard

244
00:16:05.800 --> 00:16:06.560
and go wide,

245
00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:07.380
to get

246
00:16:07.380 --> 00:16:08.060
better ideas,

247
00:16:08.060 --> 00:16:09.540
which in the end

248
00:16:09.540 --> 00:16:10.660
should solve problems better

249
00:16:10.660 --> 00:16:11.520
and make you more money.

250
00:16:13.140 --> 00:16:13.580
Prototype.

251
00:16:15.780 --> 00:16:16.820
Feel free to ask questions,

252
00:16:19.500 --> 00:16:20.060
I can answer

253
00:16:20.060 --> 00:16:20.760
while I'm talking,

254
00:16:20.800 --> 00:16:21.580
or at the end.

255
00:16:21.960 --> 00:16:22.980
We can come back to this.

256
00:16:50.420 --> 00:16:52.420
At the end there's, like, the last phase,

257
00:16:52.540 --> 00:16:53.440
actually testing,

258
00:16:53.680 --> 00:16:55.240
but before that, again, what's the idea,

259
00:16:55.320 --> 00:16:56.420
as IBM did —

260
00:16:56.560 --> 00:16:57.420
I'll tell you one,

261
00:16:57.460 --> 00:16:58.440
well, my own case.

262
00:16:59.260 --> 00:17:01.580
Again I had an idea for an app,

263
00:17:01.860 --> 00:17:02.940
and what did I do?

264
00:17:03.400 --> 00:17:05.560
You go to the App Store or Google Play

265
00:17:05.560 --> 00:17:08.860
and simply download an app

266
00:17:08.860 --> 00:17:10.820
for prototyping apps.

267
00:17:11.200 --> 00:17:12.580
I can't recall now what it's called,

268
00:17:13.120 --> 00:17:15.680
but there you can literally make

269
00:17:15.680 --> 00:17:17.100
a prototype of an app in

270
00:17:17.240 --> 00:17:18.000
well, five or six minutes.

271
00:17:20.580 --> 00:17:21.740
And then the idea — instead of:

272
00:17:21.860 --> 00:17:23.220
okay, what do I need to do now?

273
00:17:23.280 --> 00:17:24.820
Well, let's, like, allocate, I don't know,

274
